# The background-jobs chip and the JOBS list. A language server
# starting is a job until `initialize` answers; a test run is a job
# until its result lands. While one runs the statusline says so — a
# spinner and a count — and a run that FAILS leaves its words on the
# chip for ten seconds, where before it finished without a word.
#
# The server is the fake one; the `dotnet` is the corpus shim, held two
# seconds by `MNML_SHIM_DOTNET_TEST_SLEEP` so the run can be caught in
# flight. It reports one pass and one failure.
# env: PATH=${MNML_SHIMS}:${PATH}
# env: MNML_SHIM_DOTNET_TEST_SLEEP=2
write src/main.fk "fn foo() {\n  foo();\n}\n"
write .fkroot ""
write .mnml/config.zon .{ .lsp = .{ .fake = .{ .cmd = "$MNML_FAKE_LSP", .extensions = .{ "fk" }, .root_markers = .{ ".fkroot" } } } }
write Tests/Tests.csproj "<Project Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\" />\n"
write Tests/CalcTests.cs "public class CalcTests\n{\n    public void Adds() { }\n}\n"
open src/main.fk
command view.toggle_tree
expect screen contains "LSP 1"
# The server answered: its start is over, and nothing else runs, so the
# chip is gone (`ui.jobs_chip = auto`).
expect screen lacks " job "

open Tests/CalcTests.cs
command dotnet.test
expect pane tests
# In flight: the spinner, then the count.
expect screen contains " 1 job "
wait 3000
# Landed: one failed, so the job failed. The tests chip beside it
# states the result in full, so the jobs chip names the kind and leaves
# the words to it — one failure is not said three times.
expect screen lacks " 1 job "
expect screen contains " ✗ tests "
expect screen lacks "✗ tests: 1 failed"

# The list: both jobs, newest first, with how each ended.
command jobs.show
# The header owns the gap before its count, and the frame carries no
# second title.
expect screen contains "JOBS (0 running · 2 finished)"
expect screen lacks " Jobs "
expect screen contains "FINISHED (2)"
expect screen contains "✗ tests    dotnet test · 1 failed, 1 passed"
expect screen contains "✓ lsp      start fake · ready"
# Opening the list is reading the failure: the chip lets it go.
key esc
expect screen lacks "FINISHED (2)"
expect screen lacks " ✗ tests "
# The list has a chord in both profiles: `space j` (the which-key row)
# and, under standard, `Ctrl+K J`.
command editor.use_standard
key ctrl+k
key j
expect screen contains "FINISHED (2)"
key esc
command editor.use_vim
key esc
key space
key j
expect screen contains "FINISHED (2)"
key esc
